WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.674 --> 00:00:10.218
From ChatGPT to Dall-E,

00:00:10.218 --> 00:00:14.889
It might seem like the pace of progress
in artificial intelligence is unstoppable.

00:00:14.973 --> 00:00:16.474
But the artificial neural networks

00:00:16.474 --> 00:00:20.562
that underpin these programs are coming up
against some major limitations.

00:00:20.645 --> 00:00:23.523
For one, they have a hard time reasoning.

00:00:23.523 --> 00:00:26.526
Human brains
are able to reason by analogy.

00:00:26.526 --> 00:00:30.280
When we see something new,
we don't have to grow new neurons.

00:00:30.363 --> 00:00:33.867
We can generalize new concepts
from existing knowledge,

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:37.037
But artificial neural networks
struggle to reason by analogy.

00:00:37.037 --> 00:00:38.038
In this way,

00:00:38.038 --> 00:00:42.042
When confronting new information,
they often require more artificial nodes

00:00:42.042 --> 00:00:46.880
to scale up their statistical abilities,
which enables them to learn new concepts.

00:00:46.963 --> 00:00:49.632
This approach is called statistical AI.

00:00:49.632 --> 00:00:52.969
Statistically, AI is at the heart
of deep neural networks,

00:00:53.053 --> 00:00:56.692
but there is another competing approach
known as symbolic AI,

00:00:56.692 --> 00:00:58.735
which uses logic-based programing

00:00:58.735 --> 00:01:02.020
and makes use of symbols
to represent concepts and rules.

00:01:02.103 --> 00:01:04.397
So by their very nature
they are incompatible.

00:01:04.397 --> 00:01:05.315
And the big challenge

00:01:05.315 --> 00:01:09.235
is that how we can combine them together
to get the best of both worlds.

00:01:09.319 --> 00:01:12.181
An emerging approach called hyperdimensional computing

00:01:12.181 --> 00:01:13.948
aims to do just that.

00:01:13.948 --> 00:01:17.744
It taps into the power of statistical AI,
while also trying to emulate

00:01:17.744 --> 00:01:19.037
symbolic computing.

00:01:19.037 --> 00:01:22.582
It does this by using vectors,
ordered lists of numbers

00:01:22.582 --> 00:01:27.045
that can also represent arrows in space.
Depending on where they're pointing,

00:01:27.128 --> 00:01:31.758
these vectors can represent information in
a highly complex, multi-dimensional way.

00:01:31.841 --> 00:01:35.512
They can embody nuances and traits
or concepts just by small changes

00:01:35.512 --> 00:01:36.971
to their orientation.

00:01:36.971 --> 00:01:38.973
These vectors can combine to represent

00:01:38.973 --> 00:01:42.977
new concepts, then be pulled apart again
to discern how they were formed.

00:01:43.061 --> 00:01:44.062
And crucially,

00:01:44.062 --> 00:01:46.898
these hyperdimensional vectors
can encode information

00:01:46.898 --> 00:01:49.484
without having to add more nodes
to the network.

00:01:49.484 --> 00:01:52.320
The moment that we bring
hyperdimensional computing or better

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:55.782
symbolic architectures
and marry it with the statistical methods

00:01:55.782 --> 00:01:58.785
such as deep neural network,
we can have a certain advantages.

00:01:58.952 --> 00:02:03.123
In March 2023, computer scientists
at IBM Research in Zurich

00:02:03.123 --> 00:02:06.918
made a dramatic breakthrough by combining
statistical and symbolic methods

00:02:06.918 --> 00:02:10.630
to solve the Ravens progressive matrix,
a puzzle that asks an

00:02:10.797 --> 00:02:14.509
AI to predict an image to fill
the final square of a three by three grid.

00:02:14.759 --> 00:02:18.555
This is a classical problem.
The first aspects is the perception part,

00:02:18.555 --> 00:02:22.058
where we look at different visual objects
and then we have to recognize

00:02:22.225 --> 00:02:23.810
certain aspects of the objects.

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:25.937
For example,
what is the shape of the object?

00:02:25.937 --> 00:02:29.357
What is the color, what is the size?
All this kind of the property.

00:02:29.566 --> 00:02:33.987
And the second aspect is to perform
some abstract reasoning on top of these

00:02:33.987 --> 00:02:38.199
kind of attributes of the objects
to be able to solve a puzzle.

00:02:38.283 --> 00:02:41.161
So for the first time,
we combine hyperdimensional computing

00:02:41.161 --> 00:02:44.706
or better symbolic architectures
machinery, with deep neural networks

00:02:44.706 --> 00:02:48.980
to be able to solve abstract
reasoning problems at a scale.

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:52.714
Essentially, we can build inside the neural network

00:02:52.714 --> 00:02:57.802
a machinery that is able to emulate
any symbolic computing task

00:02:58.011 --> 00:03:01.014
that is hard for the neural network itself
to do.

00:03:01.055 --> 00:03:05.393
The researchers also broke new ground
by solving the problem in record speed.

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:09.063
We were able to significantly accelerate

00:03:09.147 --> 00:03:12.734
the inference times of some of abstract
reasoning tasks

00:03:12.817 --> 00:03:16.487
by 250 times, having a method
that is able to search

00:03:16.487 --> 00:03:21.743
in this kind of the space rapidly,
it's quite crucial to be able

00:03:21.743 --> 00:03:26.956
to make them quite practical for
for larger scale and real world problems.

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:29.918
Although more work is needed, researchers
like Rahimi

00:03:29.918 --> 00:03:33.796
hope that hyperdimensional computing
could be faster, more transparent,

00:03:33.922 --> 00:03:36.925
and more energy efficient than current
AI platforms.

00:03:36.966 --> 00:03:40.595
We can substantially reduce energy consumption.

00:03:40.595 --> 00:03:44.807
We are talking about, you know, ten
times, 100 times lower energy consumptions

00:03:44.849 --> 00:03:48.228
which can directly transfer to
lowering the carbon footprint

00:03:48.436 --> 00:03:51.439
once we are performing
the inference of this kind of the models.

00:03:51.606 --> 00:03:54.609
Solving driving progressive
mattresses was just a prototype.

00:03:54.734 --> 00:03:57.737
It was, so far amazing,
but the fun is just beginning,

00:03:57.737 --> 00:04:03.576
that's the beauty.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:06.246
In the 1990s, mathematician Peter

00:04:06.246 --> 00:04:10.166
Shor developed an algorithm
that threatened to break the Internet.

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:13.253
He was trying to understand
the power of quantum computers

00:04:13.336 --> 00:04:14.128
and realized

00:04:14.128 --> 00:04:18.174
that they would be able to rapidly break
large numbers into their prime factors.

00:04:18.258 --> 00:04:21.094
That would undermine
one of the central methods of modern

00:04:21.094 --> 00:04:24.847
cryptography that has safeguarded
online privacy for decades.

00:04:25.056 --> 00:04:29.352
So when I discovered the algorithm,
I just called it the factoring algorithm,

00:04:29.435 --> 00:04:32.438
the quantum factoring algorithm,
and I published it.

00:04:32.438 --> 00:04:34.941
And soon people started calling

00:04:34.941 --> 00:04:36.150
it Shor's algorithm.

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:39.821
At the heart of Shor's algorithm
is the act of repeatedly multiplying

00:04:39.821 --> 00:04:43.116
one number with itself
until the output of a certain function

00:04:43.116 --> 00:04:47.287
starts repeating. The required number
of multiplications called the functions

00:04:47.287 --> 00:04:51.374
period can be used to find
the prime factors of any large number.

00:04:51.457 --> 00:04:54.836
For the last 30 years,
this has been the fastest known algorithm

00:04:54.836 --> 00:04:56.754
for factoring integers.

00:04:56.754 --> 00:05:01.259
Then in August 2023, mathematician Oded
Regev published

00:05:01.259 --> 00:05:02.969
a groundbreaking new paper.

00:05:02.969 --> 00:05:06.597
Shor's algorithm is is great in the sense
that it allows

00:05:06.597 --> 00:05:12.395
quantum computers to factor integers
and do it quickly.

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:15.940
I was asking myself
if it can be made even faster.

00:05:16.024 --> 00:05:18.985
Regev suspected that he could speed up
Shor's algorithm

00:05:18.985 --> 00:05:23.948
by transforming the periodic function
from one dimension to multiple dimensions.

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:27.493
That meant including more numbers,
multiplying each with itself

00:05:27.493 --> 00:05:31.456
many times over to find the period
of repetition in the outputs.

00:05:31.539 --> 00:05:34.584
In doing so,
Regev established an improved method

00:05:34.584 --> 00:05:37.587
to factor integers
faster and more efficiently.

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:39.547
Shor's algorithm is about finding

00:05:39.547 --> 00:05:42.550
how long you have to walk on the line
to get back to where you started.

00:05:42.633 --> 00:05:45.678
In the new algorithm,
we no longer walk on the line.

00:05:45.803 --> 00:05:50.558
We walk on the plane
or even three dimensional space or more.

00:05:50.641 --> 00:05:52.185
the idea is that you can walk

00:05:52.185 --> 00:05:55.605
both in, right, left,
but also top down of the plane.

00:05:55.730 --> 00:05:58.983
So it could be, for instance,
that if you walk four steps to the right

00:05:58.983 --> 00:06:03.488
and five step up,
you'll be back to where you started from.

00:06:03.571 --> 00:06:05.198
Oded's improvement

00:06:05.198 --> 00:06:07.492
to my algorithm came out of the blue.

00:06:07.492 --> 00:06:11.245
There wasn't any,
you know, work leading up to it.

00:06:11.329 --> 00:06:14.749
It just had a really good idea.

00:06:14.832 --> 00:06:17.502
And he sat down, figured it out

00:06:17.502 --> 00:06:20.505
and published a paper.

00:06:20.630 --> 00:06:22.090
Like Shor's algorithm.

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:25.134
Regev's new algorithm
remains theoretical in nature.

00:06:25.301 --> 00:06:26.928
Speaking to a potential future

00:06:26.928 --> 00:06:29.459
when quantum computing becomes
more practical.

00:06:29.459 --> 00:06:33.518
I would be happy if if turns out that this new algorithm
is more practical,

00:06:33.684 --> 00:06:37.063
it has some of the components
that could make it more practical.

00:06:37.105 --> 00:06:39.399
But it's not clear yet
if it's really there.

00:06:39.399 --> 00:06:42.610
My algorithm is what, 30 years old now?

00:06:42.693 --> 00:06:47.573
And Oded found an improvement on it
that nobody had thought of before.

00:06:47.657 --> 00:06:48.741
I wouldn't be surprised

00:06:48.741 --> 00:06:54.122
if people came up with more innovative
quantum algorithms. And its quite possible

00:06:54.122 --> 00:06:57.500
that someone will improve
factoring algorithm even further.

00:06:57.583 --> 00:07:01.421
We already knew how to factor integers
using Shor's algorithm.

00:07:01.462 --> 00:07:05.550
So this new algorithm is kind of
just a speedup.

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:07.552
What I would really like to know

00:07:07.552 --> 00:07:17.854
is if quantum computers can do things
that we just didn't know how to do.

00:07:17.937 --> 00:07:20.690
Which movie do these emojis represent?

00:07:20.690 --> 00:07:22.024
When researchers asked three

00:07:22.024 --> 00:07:25.820
different large language models,
they got very different answers.

00:07:25.903 --> 00:07:26.737
The simplest

00:07:26.737 --> 00:07:29.615
AI struggled to come up
with a coherent answer.

00:07:29.615 --> 00:07:34.495
A somewhat more complex model, spit out
something reasonable but still wrong.

00:07:34.579 --> 00:07:38.291
But the most complex system nailed it in
just one try.

00:07:38.374 --> 00:07:42.503
This impressive result can be explained
by a range of exciting new abilities.

00:07:42.712 --> 00:07:45.882
AI researchers are calling
emergent behaviors.

00:07:45.965 --> 00:07:50.511
Something that is not present
in a small language model

00:07:50.511 --> 00:07:55.266
that becomes present in a larger
model can be said to have emerged.

00:07:55.349 --> 00:07:58.019
We don't think that it will be there
and then we scale it up

00:07:58.019 --> 00:08:00.271
and all of a sudden
this phenomenon is already there.

00:08:00.271 --> 00:08:04.692
So it's not necessarily
a predictable phenomenon.

00:08:04.775 --> 00:08:05.943
Emergence happens

00:08:05.943 --> 00:08:09.363
when a system behaves in ways
its individual units don't on their own.

00:08:09.614 --> 00:08:12.408
This phenomenon can be seen across nature,

00:08:12.408 --> 00:08:16.496
such as when lifeless atoms
give rise to living cells, water molecules

00:08:16.537 --> 00:08:20.666
create waves and starlings
swarm and swoop as one.

00:08:20.750 --> 00:08:24.170
Researchers are seeing that if enough
digital nodes combine in large

00:08:24.170 --> 00:08:28.007
language models, surprising
new behaviors can emerge.

00:08:28.090 --> 00:08:31.761
These behaviors are a distinct departure
from older neural networks

00:08:31.844 --> 00:08:34.889
which process language word by painstaking word.

00:08:34.889 --> 00:08:39.644
In 2017, a key piece of code
known as a transformer was introduced

00:08:39.644 --> 00:08:44.273
to LLMs, enabling neural networks
to analyze words in parallel.

00:08:44.357 --> 00:08:47.985
Now, LLMs can process
strings of text in an instant.

00:08:48.194 --> 00:08:52.740
The models we were training before
seemed to struggle with a lot of, kind of

00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:57.787
more abstract tasks, more generalization.
And what we've seen since transformers

00:08:57.787 --> 00:09:01.832
is that if we train much larger models,
they're better at those tasks.

00:09:01.999 --> 00:09:05.836
The magic of transformers
is that they're extremely good

00:09:05.836 --> 00:09:08.506
at solving sequence prediction problems.

00:09:08.506 --> 00:09:12.385
So given a bunch of items in a sequence,
predict the next thing.

00:09:12.385 --> 00:09:15.555
And this is particularly good for building
generative models

00:09:15.555 --> 00:09:19.267
of, for example, language and also images.

00:09:19.350 --> 00:09:21.936
This year, researchers
have reported a range

00:09:21.936 --> 00:09:25.273
of emergent behaviors
that enable LLMs to solve problems

00:09:25.273 --> 00:09:27.024
they've never seen before.

00:09:27.024 --> 00:09:30.695
This is known as zero shot
or few shot learning and it's long

00:09:30.695 --> 00:09:32.780
been a goal in artificial intelligence.

00:09:32.780 --> 00:09:36.367
But as exciting as the results are,
researchers still have questions

00:09:36.367 --> 00:09:39.412
about what exactly is underpinning
these emergent abilities.

00:09:39.579 --> 00:09:43.249
People are trying to figure out
at what point is there a tipping point.

00:09:43.332 --> 00:09:46.711
One theory suggests that emergence happens
when large language models

00:09:46.711 --> 00:09:50.006
break down
complex tasks into smaller steps.

00:09:50.089 --> 00:09:52.967
This process is called
chain of thought reasoning.

00:09:52.967 --> 00:09:56.846
But the true source of the tipping point
into emergence remains a mystery.

00:09:56.929 --> 00:10:00.391
The models get better as they get bigger
and people are releasing

00:10:00.391 --> 00:10:03.394
bigger models at a pretty rapid clip.

00:10:03.394 --> 00:10:07.481
Meanwhile, the science of understanding
what's happening under the hood progresses

00:10:07.481 --> 00:10:08.357
more slowly than that.

00:10:08.357 --> 00:10:11.611
So one of the challenges with emergence
is that it's unpredictable.

00:10:11.694 --> 00:10:16.449
Because on the one hand, you know, bigger
models typically mean better models.

00:10:16.449 --> 00:10:20.703
But on the other hand,
bigger models also can demonstrate

00:10:20.703 --> 00:10:24.999
novel behaviors that the developers
didn't anticipate a priori.

00:10:25.082 --> 00:10:29.629
These can be beneficial behaviors,
but they can also be harmful behaviors.

00:10:29.629 --> 00:10:33.799
So the ways in which they will be used
or are used are still unfolding.

00:10:33.924 --> 00:10:36.844
And therefore it's equally important
to measure

00:10:36.844 --> 00:10:40.723
the possible types of harms
that might be espoused in these models

00:10:40.806 --> 00:10:44.352
and how they emerge
or do not emerge with scale.

00:10:44.393 --> 00:10:47.396
And so things feel quite unpredictable
right now.

00:10:47.396 --> 00:10:49.273
But I don't think they
will always be unpredictable.

